,title,link,text,language,family
0,Mbugu language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mbugu_language,"August 29, 2025 - Maʼa is a Bantu language of Tanzania. The Mbugu people speak two divergent registers, which have been treated as separate languages by some authorities (e.g.",Mbugu,Mixed language
1,Mbugu people - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mbugu_people,"July 8, 2025 - Tanzania's Mbugu (or Ma'á) language is one of the few true hybrid languages, combining Bantu grammar with Cushitic lexicon. In actuality, the people speak two languages: one closely related to Pare and the other mixed (differing from the first mainly in the lexicon).",Mbugu,Mixed language
2,"Mbugu language. Evangelism tools, church planting resources, Christian songs and audio bible study materials",https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/mhd,"Mbugu language and dialect information. Audio Bible stories and lessons. Download free evangelism resources, MP3s, audio bible study tools, language/dialect information.",Mbugu,Mixed language
3,Mbugu - Wikidata,https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q36152,Mbugu language edit · instance of · mixed language · 0 references · language · 1 reference · stated in · Ethnologue · modern language · 1 reference · reference URL · https://iso639-3.sil.org/code/mhd · subclass of · Cushitic · 0 references · Northeast Coast Bantu ·,Mbugu,Mixed language
4,APiCS Online - Survey chapter: Mixed Ma’a/Mbugu,https://apics-online.info/surveys/62,"These analyses were based on data from earlier studies by others, dating back to Meinhof (1906). My work, Mous (2003a), is based on much more extensive fieldwork and revealed that there are actually two languages involved: a mixed language (Mixed Ma’a/Mbugu) and an ordinary unmixed Bantu language (Non-mixed Ma’a/Mbugu).",Mbugu,Mixed language
5,APiCS Online - Structure dataset 62: Mixed Ma’a/Mbugu,https://apics-online.info/contributions/62,"Speakers of Ma’á/Mbugu also speak Shambaa, the dominant language of the area, and Swahili, the national and official language of the country. Ethnologue reports 7000 speakers among 32000 ethnic Mbugu. The people are more or less concentrated in three different locations which also show some dialect variation: Rangwi, Bumbuli and Magamba. The default lect that we chose for description in APiCS is that of Magamba. ... The making of a mixed language: The case of Ma’a/Mbugu.",Mbugu,Mixed language
6,The Making of a Mixed Language: The case of Ma’a/Mbugu | Maarten Mous,https://benjamins.com/catalog/cll.26,"October 28, 2011 - Kees Versteegh, University of Nijmegen, in Studies in Language 31(4), 2007 · “This volume offers significant new insights, thanks in part to its thorough and detailed analysis. The book also marks a minor change from Mous's earlier work (e.g., Mous, 1994), where Ma'a is categorized as merely a lexical register of (Normal) Mbugu. The making of a mixed langage provides much detail, and as such it may be a relatively tough read for the uninitiated generalist.",Mbugu,Mixed language
7,"(PDF) Review of The making of a mixed language: The case of Ma'a/Mbugu, by Maarten Mous",https://www.academia.edu/10682946/Review_of_The_making_of_a_mixed_language_The_case_of_Maa_Mbugu_by_Maarten_Mous,"February 10, 2015 - The text evaluates the linguistic structures and sources of the Ma'á/Mbugu mixed language.",Mbugu,Mixed language
8,Mbugu language,https://memim.com/mbugu-language.html,"Mixed language from a Bantu language and a Cushitic language · Mbugu (proper name: Ma'a, not to be confused with Maa ) is a mixed language spoken by about 7000 people (as of 1997) is spoken in the Usambara Mountains in Tanzania. The ethnic population of Mbugu however, includes about 32,000 people",Mbugu,Mixed language
9,The Making of a Mixed Language: The Case of Ma'a/Mbugu - Maarten Mous - Google Books,https://books.google.com/books/about/The_Making_of_a_Mixed_Language.html?id=T6KqQgAACAAJ,"The Mbugu (or Ma'á) language (Tanzania) is one of the few genuine mixed languages, reputedly combining Bantu grammar with Cushitic vocabulary. In fact the people speak two languages: one mixed and one closely related to the Bantu language Pare.",Mbugu,Mixed language
